# DigiSwasthya Product Definition Document (PDD)

# Part 6 — Capability Specifications

---

# Purpose

This section fully defines every capability available within DigiSwasthya.

The previous section identified **what users should be able to do**.

This section defines **exactly how each capability behaves**.

Every capability becomes an independent product specification.

The purpose of this document is to remove ambiguity before workflows, UI design, development, or testing begin.

Each capability should be understandable without viewing any screen or implementation.

---

# Capability Specification Structure

Every capability within DigiSwasthya should be documented using the following structure.

---

## Capability Name

The business capability being defined.

---

## Purpose

Why this capability exists.

What business problem it solves.

---

## Applicable Roles

Which user roles may use this capability.

---

## Preconditions

Everything that must already be true before this capability becomes available.

Examples:

- User is logged in.
- Business is verified.
- Business is active.
- Required permissions exist.
- Required relationship exists.
- Required records exist.

If any precondition is not satisfied, the capability should not be available.

---

## Inputs

Everything required from the user or the system.

Examples include:

- Product
- Quantity
- Date
- Reason
- Amount
- Documents
- Notes

Inputs may be mandatory or optional.

---

## Validation Rules

Every input should define validation rules.

Examples:

- Required fields.
- Allowed ranges.
- Duplicate prevention.
- Quantity validation.
- Permission validation.
- Business rule validation.

Validation should occur before any business data changes.

---

## Business Behaviour

Describe exactly what happens when the capability is executed.

This section defines the business behaviour only.

It should not describe implementation.

---

## Outputs

Describe what is produced.

Examples:

- Order created.
- Invoice generated.
- Payment recorded.
- Notification sent.
- Inventory updated.

---

## Business Effects

Describe how this capability affects other business entities.

Examples:

Creating an order may affect:

- Orders
- Inventory reservation
- Notifications
- Analytics

Making a payment may affect:

- Outstanding balance
- Payment history
- Analytics

---

## Dependencies

List every dependency.

Examples:

Requires:

- Business verification
- Active connection
- Inventory
- Existing order

Without dependencies the capability cannot execute.

---

## Permissions

Define who may perform this capability.

If multiple permission levels exist, define them here.

---

## Restrictions

Describe what users are not allowed to do.

Examples:

- Cannot edit after delivery.
- Cannot exceed available quantity.
- Cannot delete financial history.

---

## Error Conditions

Describe situations where the capability cannot complete.

Examples:

- Invalid quantity.
- Insufficient inventory.
- Duplicate payment.
- Connection inactive.
- User suspended.

---

## Success Conditions

Describe what successful completion means.

---

## Audit Requirements

Define what should be recorded.

Examples:

- User
- Business
- Time
- Previous value
- New value
- Reason

---

## Related Capabilities

Reference other capabilities connected to this one.

---

# Example Capability

---

# Place Purchase Order

## Purpose

Allow a pharmacy to request products from a connected stockist.

---

## Applicable Roles

- Pharmacy

---

## Preconditions

- Pharmacy verified.
- Stockist verified.
- Active business connection.
- Products available.

---

## Inputs

- Products
- Quantities
- Delivery address
- Notes

---

## Validation Rules

- Product must exist.
- Quantity must be greater than zero.
- Duplicate products should be merged.
- Order cannot be empty.

---

## Business Behaviour

The platform creates a new purchase request linking the pharmacy, stockist, and requested products.

No inventory ownership changes at this stage.

No financial liability exists at this stage.

---

## Outputs

- New order.
- Initial order status.
- Notifications.

---

## Business Effects

Creates:

- Order

Updates:

- Order history
- Notifications
- Analytics

---

## Dependencies

Requires:

- Active connection
- Product catalogue

---

## Permissions

Only authorised pharmacy users may create purchase orders.

---

## Restrictions

Cannot order from disconnected stockists.

Cannot order while suspended.

Cannot submit an empty order.

---

## Error Conditions

- Product unavailable.
- Invalid quantity.
- Connection removed.
- Business suspended.

---

## Success Conditions

Order successfully enters the order lifecycle.

---

## Audit Requirements

Record:

- Pharmacy
- Stockist
- User
- Time
- Products
- Quantities

---

## Related Capabilities

- Browse Catalogue
- Manage Cart
- View Orders
- Track Orders

---

# Completion Criteria

Every capability identified in Part 5 should eventually receive a complete specification using this structure.

No workflow should be written until every capability has been fully specified.

User journeys should simply combine these predefined capabilities into complete business processes.

This ensures every workflow is built from consistent, reusable business behaviour rather than redefining the same logic multiple times.